## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses proficiency in low-level bit manipulation using only shifts and masks—skills essential for systems programming, embedded development, and cryptographic code. It demonstrates understanding of parallel bit-twiddling (SWAR/SIMD Within A Register) techniques.

## Key Concepts
- **Bit masking and shifting:** Extracting and repositioning specific bit ranges
- **Parallel processing:** Operating on multiple data elements (bytes) simultaneously via mask patterns
- **Byte-local vs. global reversal:** This approach reverses bits within each byte independently, unlike global bit-reversal algorithms
- **Magic constants:** The hexadecimal masks (`0xF0F0F0F0`, `0xCCCCCCCC`, `0xAAAAAAAA`) are standard patterns for these swaps

## Complete Code Example
```c
uint32 reverseBitsInEachByte32(uint32 x) {
  // Step 1: Swap adjacent nibbles (0xF0 = 11110000 in each byte)
  x = ((x & 0xF0F0F0F0) >> 4) | ((x & 0x0F0F0F0F) << 4);

  // Step 2: Swap adjacent 2-bit pairs (0xCC = 11001100 in each byte)
  x = ((x & 0xCCCCCCCC) >> 2) | ((x & 0x33333333) << 2);

  // Step 3: Swap adjacent individual bits (0xAA = 10101010 in each byte)
  x = ((x & 0xAAAAAAAA) >> 1) | ((x & 0x55555555) << 1);

  return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates mastery of fixed-popcount permutations. Tests carry propagation and ability to explain a one-shot solution without loops.

## Key Concepts
- Snoob algorithm for next with same popcount
- Carry ripple and bitwise masks
- Edge-case handling for zero or maximal permutations

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t nextBitPermutation64(uint64_t x) {
    // Assumes x != 0 and not the maximal permutation for its popcount
    uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
    uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
    uint64_t ones = x ^ ripple;
    ones = (ones >> 2) / smallest;
    return ripple | ones;
}
```

